      from two entry points; not duplicates, not fully distinct:
      - recovery_plan phase_B1 (generic liveness scanner) == sensor_omega
        stage_2c == LOCAL-RESILIENCE-NOT-WIRED-TO-ALGEDONIC. ONE build.
      - recovery_plan phase_B2 (reclassification + wiring) == sensor_omega
        stage_2d == thermal amendment thread_1+thread_2 (temperature + the
        hardware inventory's (b)-class signals wired into S3's bargain).
        ONE effort: B2 is the general principle, threads 1-2 the temperature
        instance, 2d the inventory instance.
      - recovery_plan phase_B3 (recalibration) — distinct, depends on which
        signals B2 wires; application of existing Fierro Q1-Q3 protocol.
      - recovery_plan phase_C (config centralization) == S1-ISOLATION-RELAY-ARCH
        decision_3 (config-canon) — ONE effort, not two (both docs already
        say this). Thread_4's VALUES (which io.max) are distinct from C's
        MECHANISM (one generative source) — C does not set the limits, it
        centralizes how the limits get configured.
      - recovery_plan phase_D (certification gate) — depends on thread_3
        (A5 emergency-kill ruling): D's battery includes 'kill a service under
        S3's resource bargain', which is ILLEGAL until thread_3's A5 ruling
        lands. Also depends on B1 (the liveness scanner) being certified and
        on thread_4's limits existing so the 'reallocate sanely under
        pressure' test is meaningful.
      DISTINCT content: thermal threads 1-2-3 are the Ω/A5/covenant design
      questions; recovery B1/C/D are the operational builds. Neither doc
      duplicates the other's core."

  thread_4_independence_evaluation: "thermal amendment thread_4 calls cgroup/
      IO 'the most mechanically independent, could be picked up on its own
      schedule'. EVALUATION GIVEN EVERYTHING NOW QUEUED:
      - STILL INDEPENDENT: setting cpu.max/memory.max/io.max on LXCs requires
        NO covenant/Ω/A5 decision (those govern the S3 bargain's behavior, not
        whether limits exist). It is mechanically separable from threads 1-3.
      - BUT NOT FULLY INDEPENDENT OF DISPATCH A: thread_4 touches LXC 101's
        cgroups AND the resource-governance code (s3_resource_alloc.py) whose
        crash-loop dispatch A owns. Two sub-claims: (a) the LIMITS can be set
        before the crash-loop is diagnosed — no dependency; (b) the VALIDATION
        that 'the S3 bargain responds correctly under the new limits' requires
        the crash-loop to be resolved first, AND requires thread (j) (baseline
        memory reconciliation) to land INSIDE dispatch A before any grant math
        is trusted. The amendment doc had no visibility into (j) when it wrote
        the independence claim.
      - VERDICT: thread_4 keeps its near-term mechanical independence for the
        limit-SETTING step, but its validation is sequenced AFTER dispatch A
        (which itself is paused per this dispatch's sequencing_override until
        this report is reviewed). Sequence accordingly: limits first (cheap,
        prevents recurrence), then dispatch A, then validation."

  sequenced_plan: [
    "STEP 1 — build now, no design needed (both docs agree):
       1a. recovery B1 / stage_2c generic liveness scanner (NRestarts +
           ActiveState, classification (c), single-loop restart per A5/D4).
       1b. thermal thread_4 limit-SETTING: cpu.max/memory.max/io.max on the
           running LXCs (101/104/106/107/109/110) with conservative defaults
